course: reflection
lesson: 21
title: Left out: the party, the new school, the third wheel
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Being left out feels like a verdict on your whole self: the pink envelopes for Lola's party came out in class one by one, everyone got one except Freya, and on the bus she counted: "Twenty-nine invitations. Not me. What's so wrong with me that I'm the only one?"
explain: That evening she cried in her room and wouldn't say why; the week of the party she felt sick whenever someone mentioned it; two weeks later she heard Lola's mum had only allowed so many and Lola picked her "netball girls"; it still stung, but less.
explain: What she slowly understood: it was about Lola's small group and a limit, not about everything Freya is; and handing invites out in class is unkind, even when nobody means it to be.
explain: What helped: telling Mum, a cinema trip with her own friend on the same day, and the teacher asking parents to post invitations privately in future; what didn't: checking the party photos online, which reopened the sting every time.
explain: Left-out feelings come from many places, a new school, two friends pairing up, no phone, a stammer, a different way of thinking; small steps like saying hi, joining a club or saying how you feel usually open the door again.
fact: Being left out often feels like a judgement about your whole self, but it is usually about a limit, a small group or a mistake.
fact: "Snubbed" means ignored or left out on purpose; "not everything's about you" can be a relief when the reason was something else.
fact: Looking at photos of an event you missed usually makes the hurt sharper, not smaller.
fact: New pupils often feel invisible at first; friendships take weeks or months, and clubs, buddies and saying hi speed it up.
fact: A "third wheel" is the extra person when two friends pair up; friendships change, and saying you feel left out often fixes it because the others hadn't noticed.
fact: Many families do not go on holiday abroad; a "staycation" is a holiday at home, and lying to fit in usually makes you feel worse.
fact: Feeling left out of one game can feel like being left out of everything; starting another game or joining other kids helps.
fact: FOMO means fear of missing out; being "out of the loop" means not knowing what others are talking about.
fact: A stammer is when words get stuck or repeat when speaking; letting the person finish is the kind thing to do, and speech therapists can help.
fact: Doing something different from your group, like being the only boy at dance, can feel lonely, and meeting others who share it helps.
fact: Having more than one friend helps when your only friend is away; "branch out" means make more friends.
fact: Autism is a difference in how the brain works; autistic people may find unwritten playground rules confusing, and a patient buddy can help.
exercise: Freya was the only one in her class not invited to Lola's party. What was the real reason? | Lola's mum allowed only so many and Lola picked a small group | Everybody in the class had secretly agreed that they didn't like Freya | Freya had done something terrible to Lola | Lola forgot Freya existed at all
exercise: How did Freya's feeling change over two weeks? | From crying and feeling sick to a sting that hurt less | From fine at first to much worse every single day, until she left | From happy to angry with the whole class | It never changed at all
exercise: What made Freya's hurt worse? | Checking the party photos online | Going to the cinema with her friend | Telling Mum what had happened | The teacher's new invitation rule
exercise: Why is handing out party invitations in class unkind? | Those left out see it happen in front of everyone | Invitations are not allowed to be given out in schools by law | Envelopes in class get lost too easily | It makes the party too big for the house
exercise: Leo is new and nobody has talked to him for a week. What is true? | New kids often feel invisible at first; friendships take time | He will never fit in at this school, so he should ask to move back | Everyone already decided they don't like him | He should wait silently until someone comes to him
exercise: What helped Leo? | Joining a lunchtime club and a teacher buddy | Sitting alone so nobody notices he is new | Telling everyone his old school was much better than this one | Waiting a whole year before saying hi
exercise: Aisha's two best friends started doing everything without her. What is the phrase for how she feels? | Third wheel | Fifth gear | Odd sock | Spare tyre
exercise: What helped Aisha most? | Telling them she felt left out | Sulking until they noticed | Finding new friends to make them jealous | Following them round the playground
exercise: Sam's family can't afford a holiday abroad, so he lies that he went to Spain. What happens? | He feels worse after lying | He feels better for the rest of the year | Everyone believes him and he is happy | His family gets a free trip to Spain
exercise: What is a staycation? | A holiday spent at home or nearby | A holiday where you stay up late | A trip that is cancelled at the last minute | A holiday that lasts the whole summer
exercise: Mia isn't picked for her friends' game. What helped? | Starting her own game with other kids | Sitting on the bench every break | Telling everyone she's always left out | Refusing to play any games ever
exercise: Kofi doesn't have a phone and misses the group chat jokes. What helped? | Friends filling him in at lunch | Begging for a phone every day | Pretending he got all the jokes | Ignoring his friends completely
exercise: Lily has a stammer and kids don't wait for her to finish. What is the kind thing to do? | Let her finish what she is saying | Finish her sentences for her quickly | Talk over her so she doesn't struggle | Walk away while she is speaking
exercise: Josh is teased for being the only boy in his dance class. What helped him? | Meeting other boys who dance | Quitting dance to stop the teasing | Teasing the other boys back | Dancing only when nobody's looking
exercise: Ruby's only friend is off sick for two weeks. What helped? | Joining a lunchtime club and making new friends | Hiding in the toilets every break | Phoning her friend every hour | Waiting on her own every break for her friend to come back
exercise: Theo is autistic and finds playground games confusing. Kids call him weird. What helped? | A buddy who explained the games and a quiet club | Kids laughing at him again and again until he learned the rules | Pretending to understand everything | Staying inside every playtime alone
test: Freya's mate goes, "nobody invited you? that's so cringe, you're literally Billy no-mates." ("Billy no-mates" is an unkind name for someone with no friends.) What's the truth? | It was about a limit and a small group, not about Freya | Being left out of one party means she has no friends | Freya should beg Lola for an invite next year | Freya must have done something to deserve it
test: What would happen if Freya kept refreshing the party photos all evening? | The hurt would probably get sharper again | She would feel as if she had been at the party with them too | She would get invited to the next party | It would make no difference at all
test: Leo's Canadian cousin says, "Give it time, eh, by Christmas you'll have a whole crew." ("Eh" is a Canadian way of saying right? at the end.) Is he right? | Mostly; new friendships often take weeks or months | No; if nobody talks in week one, nobody ever will | No; new kids should wait for the teacher to choose friends | Yes, but only if Leo changes his whole personality
test: Aisha's friends say, "we didn't even notice, sorry!" What does this show? | Talking about it often fixes it because others hadn't noticed | Her friends were lying and had planned it all along | She should never have told them how she felt | Friends always notice when someone feels left out
test: Sam's mate says, "we went to Majorca, it was bussin'." ("Bussin'" means really good.) Sam wants to lie. What's the better move? | Tell the truth about his staycation at home | Say he went somewhere even more expensive | Stay quiet and avoid him all September | Make fun of Majorca so he feels better
test: Mia thinks, "I'm always left out of everything. Nobody wants me." What's wrong with this thought? | Being left out of one game is not being left out of everything | It's completely true, because she wasn't picked for that one game | She should stop going to school altogether | It means she should play alone forever
test: Kofi's dad says, "Back in my day we didn't have phones and we were fine." Kofi still feels out of the loop. What helps more? | Friends filling him in at lunch | Begging for a phone every single day | Pretending he saw all the messages | Telling his friends to stop using phones
test: In a cartoon, a character with a stammer is always the joke. What's the real-life lesson? | People with a stammer need time, not laughter | Stammering is always funny and harmless to mock | People who stammer don't mind being laughed at | People who stammer should stop talking altogether
test: Josh's sister says, "lad, boys who dance are literally the coolest, ignore 'em." Is she right that he shouldn't quit? | Yes; doing what you love is worth it | No; dance is only for girls | No; he should quit dance so that he fits in with the boys | Yes, but only if he wins prizes
test: Ruby's nan from Scotland says, "Dinnae sit greetin' on yer own, hen, join a club." ("Dinnae" means don't; "greetin'" means crying; "hen" is a kind word for a girl.) What's the advice? | Join a club while her friend is off | Stay on her own until her friend comes back | Cry as much as she can to get it out | Join a club only if her friend says yes
test: Kids call Theo weird for not following the game's rules. What's the fairest view? | Autistic kids think in their own ways; explaining the rules helps | Theo is choosing to be weird to annoy them | Being different means he should be left out | He should copy everyone until he's normal
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the experience of exclusion from a social occasion? | It is frequently experienced as a judgement of one's whole self, though commonly arising from limits or small groupings | It invariably reflects the unanimous dislike of all persons concerned | It is incapable of producing any emotional effect whatsoever | It is remedied most effectively by the repeated viewing of photographs of the occasion in question until the distress subsides
exam: Identify the principal reason for which newly enrolled pupils frequently experience isolation. | Friendships require a period of time in which to develop | New pupils are prohibited from conversing with established pupils | Established pupils are required to disregard newcomers | Isolation results solely from the conduct of the new pupil
exam: It is given that a pupil experiences a stammer. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Listeners ought to permit the speaker to complete his utterance | Listeners ought to complete the speaker's sentences on his behalf | The pupil ought to refrain from speaking in class | A stammer is invariably a consequence of nervousness alone
exam: In respect of the term "staycation", determine which description is correct. | A holiday spent at or near one's home | A holiday of extended duration abroad | A holiday cancelled prior to departure | A holiday taken during school term
